> What is the "protection racket"?

It's an often debated strategy that offers some rewards at potentially high
costs, but, according to its defenders, also has the advantage of teaching you
useful tactics. In a nutshell:

Let your pet do all the fighting, but mostly run away from monsters, straight
down into the gnomish mines and to minetown, grabbing all the gold you can on
the way (possibly by stealing from shops in between). Arrive at the minetown
temple while preferrably still at XL1, not higher than XL4 in any case. Pay
the priest for protection in XL*400zm chunks. Track back up and build up XL
(and thereby HP) and equipment. This way, you end up with a strong character
with lots of HP early on. The cost, of course, is traversing the gnomish mines
with very little HP. It is true that running away and using your pet in
tactical situations is a useful skill in the early game, though.

Certain classes and races have an easier time doing the racket: The healer
starts with lots of money and the ability to heal himself and his pet; the
archaeologist can gain some money from gems and has a pickaxe.

Personally, I'd advise you to try to find the optimal speed for balancing
your HP, equipment and monster difficulty, which in the early game can be
approximated pretty well by the rule of thumb "gain one XL per DL", and focus
on armor for AC, saving the bought protection for later, when you'll have tons
of money.

